# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# New API - serverless (Vercel) configuration
#
# This is NOT a drop-in copy of .env.example. Several upstream defaults are
# actively wrong in a function runtime and are corrected below, each with the
# reason attached. See docs/VERCEL.md for the full picture.
#
# Set these in your platform's project settings, NOT in a committed file.
# vercel.json supports an `env` block; do not use it for any of these. It lives
# in the repository, and SQL_DSN contains your database password.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


# ===========================================================================
# 1. REQUIRED - the deployment does not work without these three
# ===========================================================================

# The one setting that cannot be defaulted or derived.
#
# SQLite is impossible here: the bundle is mounted read-only, and /tmp is
# per-instance and discarded when the instance is recycled, so every concurrent
# instance would see a different empty database.
#
# Prefer Postgres. The MySQL path calls checkMySQLChineseSupport, which PANICS
# unless the charset is utf8mb4 / utf8 / gbk / big5 / gb18030.
#
# Use a transaction-mode pooler host where one exists (Neon's `-pooler` host,
# PgBouncer, Supabase port 6543). Safe because chooseDB opens Postgres with
# PreferSimpleProtocol: true, which disables the implicit prepared statements
# that transaction pooling cannot support.
#
# Co-locate it with the functions -- iad1 pairs with aws-us-east-1. Every query
# crosses the network, so cross-region latency is paid on every request.
SQL_DSN=postgres://user:password@host/dbname?sslmode=require

# Authorises /api/cron, which runs the periodic jobs AND the schema migration.
#
# The endpoint fails CLOSED: if this is unset, every request to it is rejected
# rather than allowed. That is deliberate -- an open /api/cron is an
# amplification vector (it can force channel tests against every upstream
# provider) and can now also re-run DDL.
#
# Vercel sends it automatically as `Authorization: Bearer <CRON_SECRET>` on
# scheduled invocations. Use a long random string.
CRON_SECRET=

# MUST be pinned to a fixed value. The runtime now REFUSES to serve without one
# (see validateServerlessConfig), because the fallback is genuinely unsafe here
# rather than merely inconvenient.
#
# Left empty, common.SessionSecret becomes uuid.New().String() -- evaluated once
# per PROCESS. A daemon has exactly one process, so upstream can treat that as
# "a restart signs everybody out". A function runtime serves requests from many
# concurrent instances, each holding its own value, and the breakage is SILENT:
#
#   - sessions and access tokens issued by one instance are rejected by the
#     next, so users hit intermittent logouts and login loops with nothing
#     logged anywhere to explain it
#   - CRYPTO_SECRET falls back to this value, and common.GenerateHMAC keys
#     HMAC-SHA256 with it, so an HMAC written by one instance does not verify
#     on another
#
# Set CRYPTO_SECRET separately only if you want the two to differ.
#
# Do NOT use the literal string `random_string` -- common.InitEnv calls
# log.Fatal on exactly that value. On this platform a log.Fatal is an opaque
# FUNCTION_INVOCATION_FAILED with no response body, so it is much harder to
# diagnose than the JSON error you get for a missing value.
SESSION_SECRET=


# ===========================================================================
# 2. DO NOT SET THESE - the runtime configures them itself
# ===========================================================================
#
# NODE_TYPE is FORCED to "slave" by applyServerlessDefaults. Master mode would
# start daemon goroutines that the platform freezes mid-work, and would route
# router.SetRouter into SetWebRouter, which reads a web/dist embed this build
# intentionally omits.
#
# FRONTEND_BASE_URL is derived from VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL, falling back
# to VERCEL_URL (neither includes a scheme, so https:// is prepended).
#
# Set FRONTEND_BASE_URL explicitly ONLY if you serve the frontend from a
# different origin than the API, or you are behind a custom domain and want
# links in e-mails and OAuth redirects to use it. An explicit value always wins.
#
# FRONTEND_BASE_URL=https://your-custom-domain.com


# ===========================================================================
# 3. CONNECTION POOL - the most common way to take the database down
# ===========================================================================
#
# Upstream defaults are SQL_MAX_OPEN_CONNS=1000 and SQL_MAX_IDLE_CONNS=100,
# sized for ONE long-lived process. Here the pool is per INSTANCE, so the real
# ceiling is 1000 x concurrent instances. A modest traffic spike exhausts any
# managed database's connection limit and it starts refusing connections --
# including to your dashboard.
#
# Keep these small. A function serves one request at a time, so a handful of
# connections per instance is genuinely enough.
SQL_MAX_OPEN_CONNS=3
SQL_MAX_IDLE_CONNS=1

# Short lifetime so a frozen instance does not hold a connection the database
# has already given up on.
SQL_MAX_LIFETIME=30


# ===========================================================================
# 4. MUST BE DISABLED - silent data loss otherwise
# ===========================================================================
#
# Both of these buffer records in process memory and flush them on a timer. In a
# runtime that freezes instances between requests and discards them without
# warning, the timer may never fire again -- so the buffered rows are simply
# lost. For BATCH_UPDATE that is billing and quota data.
BATCH_UPDATE_ENABLED=false
DATA_EXPORT_ENABLED=false


# ===========================================================================
# 5. TIMEOUTS - fail with a real error instead of a truncated stream
# ===========================================================================
#
# maxDuration is 300s (Hobby's default AND maximum). When the platform kills a
# function mid-response the client sees a truncated stream rather than an error,
# so these are set just below the ceiling to let New API fail first with a
# proper message where it can.
RELAY_TIMEOUT=280
STREAMING_TIMEOUT=280


# ===========================================================================
# 6. CLIENT IP - do NOT trust every proxy
# ===========================================================================
#
# LEAVE TRUSTED_PROXIES UNSET.
#
# The upstream default already trusts loopback, RFC 1918 and IPv6 ULA
# (127.0.0.0/8, ::1, 10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16, fc00::/7), which covers the
# platform's internal hop, because the function process is invoked from a local
# proxy rather than from the public internet.
#
# TRUSTED_PROXIES=0.0.0.0/0 looks like a tidy way to say "trust the edge". It is
# not -- it is a client IP spoofing hole, and an earlier revision of this file
# wrongly recommended it.
#
# Gin walks X-Forwarded-For from right to left and returns the first entry it
# does NOT trust. When every entry is trusted the loop runs out and falls
# through to items[0]: the LEFTMOST value, which is whatever the client sent.
# So a request carrying
#
#     X-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.4
#
# makes c.ClientIP() report 1.2.3.4. Every control keyed on client IP then
# becomes attacker-controlled:
#
#   - token IP allowlists (token.GetIpLimits, checked in TokenAuth) can be
#     bypassed outright -- this is an access control, not just throttling
#   - the IP rate limiters (GW / GA / CT, upload, download) can be bypassed by
#     rotating the header value on each request
#   - the e-mail verification limiter (2 per 30s per IP) stops limiting, turning
#     that endpoint into a mail bomber
#   - Turnstile is handed a forged `remoteip`, weakening its risk scoring
#   - audit logs record attacker-chosen addresses, so abuse cannot be traced
#
# Widen it only if you put your OWN proxy in front of the deployment, and then
# list that proxy's addresses explicitly instead of 0.0.0.0/0.
#
# TRUSTED_PROXIES=
#
# TRUSTED_PROXIES=none is also wrong here: Gin would ignore X-Forwarded-For
# completely, so every client collapses into the platform's internal address and
# a single user's traffic exhausts everybody's rate limit.


# ===========================================================================
# 7. REDIS - effectively required, not a performance option
# ===========================================================================
#
# Without Redis every limiter falls back to inMemoryRateLimiter, which lives in
# per-instance memory. Instances scale horizontally and are recycled freely, so
# the effective limit becomes (configured limit x live instances) and resets
# whenever an instance is replaced. In practice the deployment then has no
# working rate limiting at all. ModelRequestRateLimit has the same problem.
#
# Redis also shrinks the config-staleness window left by the dropped SyncOptions
# and SyncChannelCache goroutines.
#
# Use a serverless-friendly Redis (Upstash and similar) -- a TCP connection pool
# per instance has the same fan-out problem as the database.
# REDIS_CONN_STRING=


# ===========================================================================
# 8. COOKIES - handled automatically, but worth understanding
# ===========================================================================
#
# applySessionCookieDefaults sets SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE=true and derives
# SESSION_COOKIE_TRUSTED_URL from the resolved FRONTEND_BASE_URL, so neither
# normally needs a value here. Upstream defaults the flag to false to keep local
# http development working; this platform is HTTPS-only, so that trade-off does
# not apply.
#
# The flag is not just a cookie attribute. middleware.SessionCookieOriginGuard
# disables ITSELF when it is off:
#
#     if !common.SessionCookieSecure {
#         c.Next()
#         return
#     }
#
# That guard is the Origin/Referer check protecting the cookie-authenticated
# /api/user/auth/refresh and /api/user/auth/logout endpoints, so leaving the flag
# off on a public site means those endpoints have no CSRF defence at all.
#
# The two variables must be set TOGETHER. SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE=true with an
# empty SESSION_COOKIE_TRUSTED_URL makes InitSessionCookieSettings return an
# error, which common.InitEnv turns into log.Fatal -- another opaque
# FUNCTION_INVOCATION_FAILED. Trusted URLs must be https, and scheme+host only:
# no path, no query, no credentials.
#
# Override only when the frontend lives on a different origin, and then list
# every browser origin that may call the auth endpoints:
#
# SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE=true
# SESSION_COOKIE_TRUSTED_URL=https://app.example.com,https://admin.example.com
#
# An explicit SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE=false is still honoured, for a deployment
# sitting behind a proxy that terminates TLS somewhere else.


# ===========================================================================
# 9. DEBUGGING
# ===========================================================================
#
# pprof registers HTTP handlers and expects a long-lived process; it has no
# meaning per-request and exposes profiling endpoints.
ENABLE_PPROF=false

# DEBUG=false
# GIN_MODE=release


# ===========================================================================
# 10. DELIBERATELY OMITTED
# ===========================================================================
#
# SQLITE_PATH             - see SQL_DSN. Setting this would appear to work and
#                           then lose every write, which is worse than failing.
# PORT                    - nothing listens on a port; the platform invokes a
#                           handler directly.
# SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT_SECONDS- there is no graceful shutdown hook to honour.
# LOG_DIR                 - does not exist. Log output is controlled by the
#                           --log-dir FLAG, which the bootstrap clears because
#                           /var/task is read-only and InitEnv would log.Fatal
#                           trying to mkdir it. Logs go to stdout/stderr, which
#                           the platform already collects.
# SYNC_FREQUENCY,         - the goroutines that consumed these were dropped;
# CHANNEL_UPDATE_FREQUENCY  see the goroutine triage table in docs/VERCEL.md.


# ===========================================================================
# 11. FIRST RUN
# ===========================================================================
#
#   1. Set the three required variables above, then redeploy so the functions
#      pick them up. Environment changes do NOT apply to existing deployments.
#
#   2. Create the schema. Slave nodes skip migration on boot, so this is not
#      optional on a fresh database:
#
#        curl -i "https://<deployment>/api/cron?job=migrate" \
#             -H "Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET"
#
#      Safe to repeat -- AutoMigrate only adds what is missing. Re-run it after
#      any upgrade that adds migrations.
#
#   3. Open /setup to create the first admin account. A migrated-but-empty
#      database is the correct starting state: createRootAccountIfNeed() is not
#      called by InitDB(), so CheckSetup() serves the first-run wizard instead.
#
#   4. Verify:
#
#        curl -i https://<deployment>/api/status
#        curl -i https://<deployment>/v1/models -H "Authorization: Bearer <key>"
#
# Note on cron: Hobby allows ONE invocation per DAY, with only hour-level
# precision. The Midjourney and async-task handlers want to poll every 15
# SECONDS, so async job completion will not be picked up promptly. Either move
# to Pro (once per minute) or drive /api/cron from an external scheduler with
# the same Bearer secret -- the endpoint is deliberately platform-agnostic.
